# One origin, two surfaces (ADR-0010, ADR-0011).
#
# The split has to be invisible from the outside: a citation URL 307s to /app or
# /api/v1, and a redirect that crossed origins would break the Accept-based
# routing, cookies, and anything a browser scopes to a host. So a proxy owns the
# port, and the two services behind it never need to know about each other.
#
# This is also what makes the surfaces independently deployable, which was the
# point of separating them: /app can be rebuilt, rolled back, or replaced with a
# static export without the API restarting, and vice versa.
#
# $SITE_ADDRESS is the site address. It defaults to :8000, which is the dev
# stack: plain HTTP, no host matching. In production it is a hostname
# (`uscode.example.org`), and Caddy provisions and renews the certificate for it
# automatically — that one substitution is the whole HTTPS story.

{
	# Caddy hands X-Forwarded-Proto to the API, and uvicorn is started with
	# --proxy-headers so `request.url.scheme` is the *client's* scheme rather
	# than the http of this hop. That is what decides whether the session cookie
	# gets `Secure` (api/auth.py), so it is a security setting, not a cosmetic
	# one — and USC_COOKIE_SECURE=true in production means a regression here
	# cannot silently downgrade the cookie anyway.
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static private_ranges
	}
}

{$SITE_ADDRESS::8000} {
	encode gzip zstd

	# `header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}` *overwrites* rather than appends,
	# so the header the backends read is always the real peer of this hop.
	#
	# It has to be done here, and it is not a belt-and-braces measure. Both
	# compose files start uvicorn with `--forwarded-allow-ips "*"`, and in that
	# mode uvicorn's proxy-headers middleware returns
	# `x_forwarded_for_hosts[0]` — the *leftmost*, entirely client-supplied
	# value; the reverse scan that finds the first untrusted hop only runs when
	# trust is not `*`. Astro's Node adapter reads the leftmost value too
	# (`astro/dist/core/app/node.js:121`), so both surfaces have the same shape.
	#
	# The precondition is the `trusted_proxies` setting above, and it is worth
	# stating exactly, because the mechanism is not "Caddy always appends":
	# Caddy preserves an inbound X-Forwarded-For only when the peer is a trusted
	# proxy, and replaces it otherwise. `static private_ranges` therefore means
	# **any peer on a private network is trusted to name its own client**.
	#
	# Measured, with a Caddy 2 container and an echo upstream
	# (docs/verification/xff.md):
	#
	#   trusted_proxies private_ranges, no header_up:
	#       curl -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4'  ->  "1.2.3.4, 192.168.65.1"
	#   the same, with header_up:
	#       curl -H 'X-Forwarded-For: 1.2.3.4'  ->  "192.168.65.1"
	#
	# So the forged value really does land leftmost and win, and uvicorn really
	# does read it — but only from a peer inside a private range. Facing the
	# internet directly, as ADR-0020 deploys it, client peers are public and
	# Caddy already discards what they send. What was exposed is the dev stack,
	# and any future shape with a CDN, load balancer or sidecar in front — which
	# ADR-0018 explicitly anticipates. That made the per-IP login throttle
	# (api/auth.py, ADR-0019) bypassable with one header and `login_attempts.ip`
	# an attacker-controlled write, in exactly the deployment shape most likely
	# to come next.
	#
	# Overwriting closes it unconditionally, and independently of the trust
	# setting — which is the property worth having, since the setting is remote
	# from the code that relies on it. Narrowing `--forwarded-allow-ips` to a
	# literal range instead was rejected: the compose network's CIDR is assigned
	# by Docker and changes. See ADR-0029.
	# robots.txt is a property of the *host*, not of either surface, and this
	# file is the only thing that owns the host — so serving it here is what
	# makes one answer true for /app and /api/v1 alike, including for a request
	# that arrives while one of them is restarting.
	#
	# It exists because the site was measured being crawled into the ground.
	# One hour of the proxy log on 2026-08-03: 43,068 requests, of which 33,937
	# were ClaudeBot and 9,079 GPTBot — 99.9% — against roughly 48 from human
	# browsers. 36,465 of them (85%) carried `?release=`, which is the tell:
	# the crawlers had found the version dimension, and the space behind it is
	# 65,938 sections x 382 release points, about 25 million reader pages,
	# before counting the 96,185,732 `?id=` guids that also resolve. There is no
	# crawl budget that finishes that, so the load has no natural end — the box
	# sat at load average 2.06 on 2 vCPUs with nobody reading it, which is what
	# kept `uscode-cpu-credits-low` in alarm and, on a t4g in unlimited mode,
	# what was being paid for.
	#
	# The site had no robots.txt at all: five requests for one in that hour, all
	# 404. So this is not a tightening of a policy, it is the first statement of
	# one, and it is deliberately the blunt version while the demo is being
	# shown to the audience it was built for. `Disallow: /` is advisory and only
	# binds well-behaved crawlers — which is exactly what the measured traffic
	# is, both bots declaring themselves and both honouring robots.txt.
	# Shaping the crawl instead (index the ~66k current-text sections, disallow
	# the permutation space) is the setting to come back to when the site should
	# be discoverable; it is a change to this block and nothing else.
	handle /robots.txt {
		header Content-Type "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
		respond <<ROBOTS
			User-agent: *
			Disallow: /
			ROBOTS 200
	}

	handle /app* {
		reverse_proxy frontend:4321 {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		}
	}

	# Everything else is FastAPI: /api/v1, the citation redirector at /us/usc,
	# /health, and the OpenAPI docs.
	handle {
		reverse_proxy api:8001 {
			header_up X-Forwarded-For {remote_host}
		}
	}

	# Cache-Control is the origin's decision, never this file's: whether a
	# response may be kept forever depends on whether the release point was
	# pinned, which only the resolver knows (params.py, ADR-0018). Caddy passes
	# those headers through untouched and adds no cache of its own — it has no
	# built-in one, and the immutable headers are what a CDN in front would read
	# if one is ever added.
	# Browser security headers (ADR-0030). `?` sets a header only if the origin
	# did not, so a route can still speak for itself; none currently does.
	header {
		# Sent always; browsers are required to ignore it when it arrives over
		# plain HTTP (RFC 6797 §8.1), so the dev stack on :8000 is unaffected.
		#
		# `includeSubDomains` is a real commitment rather than a free upgrade:
		# every subdomain of $SITE_ADDRESS must then be HTTPS for as long as the
		# max-age, including ones that do not exist yet. Accepted because this is
		# a single-host deployment (ADR-0020), and a plain-HTTP sibling of a site
		# serving law is not something to leave room for.
		?Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		# The reader renders statutory XML into HTML; these cost nothing and
		# close the obvious own-goals.
		?X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		?Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"

		# Nothing here has any reason to be framed, and being framed is how a
		# reader gets clickjacked into a watchlist change or a logout. Both
		# spellings: `frame-ancestors` below is the one that counts,
		# X-Frame-Options is for anything predating it.
		?X-Frame-Options "DENY"

		# The site loads no third-party anything — no CDN, no web font, no
		# analytics, which is downstream of ADR-0011's no-bundle choice. So
		# `default-src 'self'` is not a restriction that had to be negotiated,
		# it is a description of what the site already does, and that makes it
		# worth enforcing as the backstop under the reader's `set:html` sinks.
		#
		# `script-src` must carry 'unsafe-inline' for now, and saying so plainly
		# is better than implying the policy is tighter than it is: every island
		# is `<script is:inline>` — eight of them — which is ADR-0022's
		# deliberate no-framework choice, and the theme toggle in particular has
		# to run inline before first paint or the page flashes light before going
		# dark (ADR-0027). Nonces are the fix, and they need the Astro middleware
		# ADR-0029 has just added, so that is a follow-up with somewhere to go
		# rather than a wish. `style-src` is the same story: USWDS ships bundled
		# CSS, but a handful of components still carry `style="…"` attributes.
		#
		# `worker-src 'self' blob:` is here for exactly one thing: ReDoc builds
		# its search index in a worker created from a Blob, and with no
		# `worker-src` the browser falls back to `script-src`, which has no
		# `blob:` — so `/redoc` rendered in full and its search box silently
		# returned nothing for every query (measured, ADR-0032). Naming the
		# directive is what keeps the widening *narrow*: it permits a worker
		# from a blob and changes nothing about what may execute in the page,
		# which is the part `script-src` governs and which stays as it was.
		#
		# The reader itself has no worker of any kind, so this buys the reader
		# nothing and costs it nothing — it is a docs-page allowance that has to
		# live in a site-wide header because there is one header. The
		# alternative was leaving a documented page half-broken to keep a
		# directive absent, which is a worse trade than it sounds: an absent
		# directive is not a stricter one, it is `script-src` doing double duty.
		?Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; worker-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'"
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
